\documentclass[a4paper]{article}
%\VignetteIndexEntry{Prime Number Sieve}
%\VignettePackage{numTheory}

\title{Prime Number Generation using prime_sieve}
\author{Siddhesh Akole}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

This vignette demonstrates the \code{prime_sieve} function from the \pkg{numTheory} package.
The function generates all prime numbers up to a given limit using the Sieve of Eratosthenes,
an efficient algorithm for prime number generation.

\section{Inputs}
The function takes one integer input:
- \code{n}: the upper bound up to which prime numbers are generated.

\section{Example}
Here we generate prime numbers up to 30:

<<echo=TRUE, results=TRUE>>=
library(numTheory)

prime_sieve(30)
@

\section{Constraints}
In R, exact integer range is: \code{- .Machine\$integer.max} to \code{.Machine\$integer.max}.  
For doubles, the representable range is: \code{.Machine\$double.xmin} to \code{.Machine\$double.xmax}.  


\section{Edge Cases}
- \code{n < 2}: returns an empty vector (no primes below 2).  
- \code{n = 2}: returns \code{2} as the only prime.  
- Large \code{n}: performance depends on memory, but algorithm remains efficient.

<<echo=TRUE, results=TRUE>>=
# Edge case: n < 2
prime_sieve(1)

# Edge case: n = 2
prime_sieve(2)
@

\section{Complexity}
The \code{prime_sieve} function uses the Sieve of Eratosthenes, which runs in 
\(\mathcal{O}(n \log \log n)\) time, making it efficient for generating primes 
up to large limits.

\section{Summary}
The \code{prime_sieve} function provides a fast and reliable way to generate prime numbers,
useful for number theory, cryptography, and algorithmic applications.

\end{document}
